# PRIME Backstage Portal

Strict guidelines for this Nuxt 3 prototype. Read `docs/project-context.md` before changing code.

## Tech Stack & Standards

- Use the Vue 3 Composition API with `<script setup lang="ts">`.
- Use strictly typed TypeScript. Do not use `any`. Type props, emits, store state, and mock records.
- Rely on Nuxt 3 auto-imports for composables, components, and utilities (`ref`, `computed`, `useSessionStore`, `navigateTo`). Use explicit imports only for types (`import type`).
- Style with Tailwind CSS utility classes. Do not add a second styling system or new global CSS unless a utility cannot express the rule.

```vue
<script setup lang="ts">
const session = useSessionStore()
</script>
```

## State Management

- Use Pinia exclusively for application state and all mocked backend data.
- Do not keep global concepts in local component state. That includes user roles, the signed-in user, activity codes, event modes, session timers, and the active portal tab.
- Local `ref` or `reactive` state is only for view-local UI, such as an unsubmitted input or an expanded panel.

```ts
// Global session facts belong in the store.
session.setEventMode('teams')

// A draft the user has not submitted may stay local.
const draft = ref('')
```

## Component Structure

- Break complex views into small, reusable components in `/components`.
- Keep pages thin: route, read the store, and compose components.
- Put role-specific tool views under `/components/views`.

## Continuous Documentation

Before beginning any new task, you must read `docs/project-context.md` to understand the current state. Upon completing any architectural change, new page, or state modification, you MUST update `docs/project-context.md` to reflect the changes.
